diff --git a/docs/deployment.md b/docs/deployment.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..ccfeecc
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/deployment.md
@@ -0,0 +1,41 @@
+# Deploy Fizzy with Kamal
+
+This setup runs Fizzy and its Solid Queue worker in one app container. Kamal Proxy handles HTTPS. You don't need a separate database, job server, or registry account.
+
+## Prepare the server
+
+Point a public DNS hostname at your Linux server. Allow inbound TCP ports 80 and 443 for the proxy and certificate issuance. Make sure you can SSH to the server and run Docker there. Kamal uses `root` by default; set `ssh.user` in the deploy config if you use a different Docker-enabled user.
+
+## Configure your fork
+
+On your development machine, have Docker with Buildx running. Use the Ruby version in `.ruby-version` and run `bundle install` in your open-source checkout.
+
+Edit [`config/deploy.yml`](../config/deploy.yml):
+
+- Replace `fizzy.example.com` after `&host` with your hostname (no `https://`). The proxy and Rails reuse that value.
+- Set `SMTP_ADDRESS` and `SMTP_PORT` to your mail provider's STARTTLS endpoint, and `MAILER_FROM_ADDRESS` to a sender it allows. Fizzy sends sign-in codes by email.
+- If the server is ARM64, change `builder.arch` to `arm64`.
+
+Create a local secrets file:
+
+```sh
+cp .kamal/secrets.example .kamal/secrets
+chmod 600 .kamal/secrets
+bin/rails secret
+```
+
+Paste the generated value into `SECRET_KEY_BASE` and fill in `SMTP_USERNAME` and `SMTP_PASSWORD` in `.kamal/secrets`. Keep the key across deploys so sessions and signed links stay valid. Never commit this file.
+
+For optional browser push, [generate a VAPID key pair](development.md#web-push-notifications) and add both variables to `.kamal/secrets` and to `env.secret` in the deploy config.
+
+## Deploy
+
+Commit your non-secret changes (Kamal builds from Git), then run:
+
+```sh
+bin/kamal setup
+```
+
+Kamal builds the image locally and makes it available to the server over SSH. The entrypoint prepares the databases on startup. Open `https://your-hostname` and sign up for the first account. To check the running release, visit `https://your-hostname/up` or run `bin/kamal app logs`.
+
+For updates, commit the changes and run `bin/kamal deploy`. Each release mounts the same `fizzy_storage` Docker volume at `/rails/storage`. It holds all four SQLite databases (primary, queue, cache and cable) and uploaded files. Keep the volume when replacing containers. A volume is not a backup: take consistent off-server backups of it and keep your secrets separately.
